/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9cc186b-22af-55d8-bf89-1dd19ae7b90f" :question/math-academy-id "q-164154" :question/problem "![](images/81/81604581fae8b25776727eca4450caa7d48c42a781706455ed585d5cfc4a25fa.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $A$ and $C.$ Hence $A$ and $C$ are local maximum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $A({x}_{A},{y}_{A},{z}_{A}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{A},{y}_{A})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$, the point $A$ does satisfy the conditions for a local maximum.\n\n![](images/36/3651b43178819666002775a26071a0bd2c9cb48b58a3c5516c38e085d50fc4a6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124647 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fb118f-c1cc-51fd-a8cc-c29cf705acfe" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "118339a7-669b-5081-8175-449ccce667f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function does not have local maxima" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445192} {:answer/id #uuid "80185868-d8d6-5a9a-9b8a-83d741afd936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445193} {:answer/id #uuid "b251a48a-e4be-5932-a217-f573cfba9f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445194} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b3e9e9-2073-5961-8289-000100206e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445195} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b33820-f35d-5434-abf3-958f348e4695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2b33820-f35d-5434-abf3-958f348e4695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445196} :answer-field/id #uuid "261ceadd-9e63-5d86-9762-c5e81f0679da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da2e1b4b-fd99-52e9-91fa-fb0d43968219" :question/math-academy-id "q-164157" :question/problem "![](images/19/19f4f2d616fa648fcd4663be22b32ab18662fffa8e31f9bd44f0dd3b6da99208.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch region exists for the point $A.$ Hence, $A$ is a local maximum point.\n\n**Note**: For the point $A({x}_{A},{y}_{A},{z}_{A}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{A},{y}_{A})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$, the point $A$ does satisfy the conditions for a local maximum.\n\n![](images/f0/f0b16c2cb372280ec39915b0e8d4e396e68baec9a8e20edc5389ced079648836.png)"}]}
